nodecode-ichor is a rewrite of mystiquemide's mystiquemide/ichor:

  https://github.com/mystiquemide/ichor
  commit e204b791a23a2495ef556a26b5627b5511290e3c
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Mystique Mide (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the correction detector (the rejecting openers, the redirect phrases
and their guards, and the re-sent message that is a retry pair), the local
scrubber and its counts, the local mistake labels, the card text, the store (one
JSONL file under the home instead of a SQLite database of its own, with the same
pending / kept / shared / discarded statuses and a discarded pair keeping no
text), the /ichor verbs (status, review, keep, share, discard, export, share,
set repo / public / private, off, on, off --global, forget-all), the TRL
conversational export and the Atropos pairs file, and the optional upload to a
Hugging Face dataset with the token read from the environment and sent only to
huggingface.co. Hermes published a post_llm_call hook with the turn's text;
Nodecode publishes the turn's own facts, so the rewrite reads the completed
turn from the event log through the :FRAME point, and its own verbs answer
strings an eval form can call.

Left out: the Atropos environment and reward modules (the export writes their
pairs.jsonl and names the folder, but ships no Python), the one-character card
replies claimed off the gateway's admission hook (k / s / d now settle a pair
through /ichor keep, share and discard), the in-place edit of a posted card on
Telegram, Discord and Slack, and the optional side-question judge that asked the
session's own agent for a two-to-four-word label -- the local labels from the
operator's words stand in its place, so no extra model call is made.
